","To calculate the entropy change (ΔS) of the reaction, we can use the following formula:

ΔS = ΣS_products - ΣS_reactants

where S_products and S_reactants are the molar entropies of the product and reactant products, respectively.

Given the reaction:
\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) \]

With the molar entropies:
\[ \text{S(PbS)} = 91.2 \, \text{J/K mol} \]
\[ \text{S(O}_2\text{)} = 205.1 \, \text{J/K mol} \]
\[ \text{S(PbO)} = 66.5 \, \text{J/K mol} \]
\[ \text{S(SO}_2\text{)} = 248.2 \, \text{J(K mol} \]

Now, we can calculate the entropy change:

ΔS = (2 * S(PbO) + 2 * S(SO2)) - (2 * S(PbS) + 3 * S(O2))

ΔS = (2 * 66.5 + 2 * 248.2) - (2 * 91.2 + 3 * 205.1)

ΔS = (133 + 496.4) - (182.4 + 615.3)

ΔS = 629.4 - 797.7

ΔS = -168.3 \, \text{J/K mol}

The entropy change of the reaction is -168.3 J/K mol.",relevance,"# Task Description
