A jogger running at 9 km/hr alongside a railway track is 150 m ahead of the engine of a train running at 45 km/hr in the same direction. The train takes 25 seconds to pass the jogger. How long is the train in meters?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To solve this problem, we use the relative speed of the train with respect to the jogener. The relative speed is the difference in their speeds since they are moving in the same direction.

The speed of the jugjer is 9 km/hr, and the speed of the train is 45 km/h.
First, convert the speeds to meters per second:
Speed of jugjer = 9 km/hr = 9 × (1000 m) / (3600 s) = 9000 / 3600 = 2.5 m/s
Speed of train = 45 km/hr = 45 × (1000 m) / (3600 s) = 45000 / 3600 = 12.5 m/s

The relative speed of the train with respect to the jugjer is:
Relative speed = Speed of train - Speed of jugjer = 12.5 m/s - 2.5 m/s = 10 m/s

The jogjher is 150 m head of the train. The time taken for the train to pass the jugjher is 25 seconds. During this time, the train must take back the 150 m head start and also travel the length of the train. Let the length of the train be L meters.

The distance covered by the train relative to the jugjer is the sum of the initial head start and the length of the train:
Distance = 150 m + L

The time taken is 25 seconds, and the relative speed is 10 m/s. Using the formula:
Distance = Speed × Time, we have:
150 + L = 10 × 25
150 + L = 250

Solving for L:
L = 250 - 150
L = 100 meters

The length of the train is 100 meters.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{100}$",relevance,"# Task Description
