"You’re pulling a chest of drawers, applying a force of 60.0 N at an angle of 60.0°. How much work do you do pulling it over 10.0 m?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
You’re pulling a chest of drawers, applying a force of 60.0 N at an angle of 60.0°. How much work do you do pulling it over 10.0 m?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the work done, we use the formula:  
\[
W = F \cdot d \cdot \cos \theta
\]  
where:  
- \( W \) is the work done (in Joules)  
- \( F \) is the force applied (in Newtons)  
- \( d \) is the distance traveled (in meters)  
- \( \theta \) is the angle between the force and the direction of displacement (in degrees)  
Given:  
- \( F = 60.0 \, \text{N} \)  
- \( d = 10.0 \, \text{m} \)  
- \( \theta = 60.0^\circ \)  
Substituting the values:  
\[
W = 60.0 \, \text{N} \cdot 10.0 \, \text{m} \cdot \cos 60.0^\circ
\]  
Since \( \cos 60^\circ = 0.5 \):  
\[
W = 600 \, \text{N} \cdot \text{m} \cdot 0.5 = 300 \, \text{N} \cdot \text{m} = 300 \, \text{J}
\]  
Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{300 \, \text{J}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
